{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.85,"content_digest":"565c387494288f4082de6309d84c43f837c7c4dad7c10947b28b9a28845141a1","created_at":"2026-08-22T03:33:24+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[],"link_id":"foundation-link:5ed2f09a19ee7dc34af939e0","meta_principle_id":"meta:statistics-causality:interference","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:general:01KZZ95TN39X3A8Q4VKF45VRNB","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The meta-principle establishes that units can causally affect one another's outcomes. In multi-agent environments where actions alter others' rewards and state evolution, modeling agents as independent single agents ignores this causal interference, producing non-stationarity and suboptimal solutions.","relation_type":"supports","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-22T04:12:58+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0KTQZ6M68SKHPKARZR7EKFP"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T03:33:24+00:00","variable_compatibility":"partial"}
{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.65,"content_digest":"111f705b952338867bf07e70372caaa7232e0d6bab08a07acb7baa2fb40a029a","created_at":"2026-08-22T16:37:26+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"ae8632fef55b86475560787e303179b22278b6f5962018daafea7f0d084c6c39","input_tokens":4647,"latency_ms":33543,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"a8bcf440dad457c9ee4a5c4a62d9f33ee4757d27ec0c1530060a34867ffc0bbc","output_tokens":2492,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:9374c04289e3ff61143c0167","meta_principle_id":"meta:economics-game-theory:bounded-rationality","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:ai-ml:01M0N5A4CY7MS8F3TNTTSNK0M5","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"Bounded rationality holds that decision makers operate under limited attention and computation, and that interfaces shape outcomes by changing the effective decision problem. The principle applies this idea to conversational BPMN generation: requiring users to segment and reassemble a long process description imposes avoidable cognitive costs, so system-supported progressive disclosure changes the task structure to reduce load and improve modeling outcomes.","relation_type":"consistent_with","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-22T17:07:53+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0N72WTZDNS3VF5QZJB70PZ5"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T16:37:26+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.72,"content_digest":"2a7f045c3726b06ca8d994824b9f02ada0362a4b3fbfd9b4091a71a7bd727534","created_at":"2026-08-22T14:35:51+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"1fbe591198af42f04ecc4708a6b345fb925cddc902b7bb2a0771383e2ea32c75","input_tokens":6266,"latency_ms":40605,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"556517956d5013048a837743676c51b26129d9dcf7f156f2890a4356aa40bd75","output_tokens":2572,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:beaa0fd03342173b4d2bb4a0","meta_principle_id":"meta:foundations:robustness-invariance","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:neuroscience-cognition:01M0MYB98R9KRZ2MXJEKVCCAGY","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The principle's core claim includes persistence of the negative flexibility–modularity correlation across multiple functional and anatomical parcellations and against randomized-signal controls, which is the kind of invariance across representation choices that the robustness-invariance meta-principle describes. The link is epistemic consistency, not a derivation of the empirical association.","relation_type":"consistent_with","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-22T17:07:53+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0N72WTZDNS3VF5QZJB70PZ5"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T14:35:51+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
